      "Abstract": {
        "CopyrightInformation": "© American Academy of Otolaryngology\u2014Head and Neck Surgery Foundation 2016.",
        "AbstractText": [
          {
            "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
            "Label": "OBJECTIVES",
            "content": "(1) Correlate skull base thickness with perioperative outcomes for spontaneous cerebrospinal fluid (CSF) otorrhea. (2) Augment perioperative counseling of patients with abnormally thin skull bases."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "STUDY DESIGN",
            "content": "Case series with chart review."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "SETTING",
            "content": "Tertiary center. Patients with spontaneous CSF otorrhea have thin skull bases. This is associated with obesity and/or idiopathic intracranial hypertension. The influence of skull base thinning on perioperative outcomes is unknown."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "SUBJECTS AND METHODS",
            "content": "A retrospective review was conducted from 2004 to 2014. Forty-eight cases of spontaneous CSF otorrhea met the inclusion criteria of primary surgery by the senior authors: preoperative dedicated temporal bone computed tomography, absence of other leak etiologies, and follow-up >6 months. Patients were stratified into thin (<0.9 mm) and thick (>0.9 mm) groups based on computed tomography measures of their tegmen. Primary outcomes measures were as follows: postoperative meningitis, recurrent leak, second site leak (contralateral ear/anterior fossa), and permanent shunt placement. Hearing outcomes were not assessed in this study."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
            "Label": "RESULTS",
            "content": "Thirty and 15 patients composed the thin and thick groups, respectively. Both the incidence (P < .0001) and the rate (P = .005) of adverse outcomes were significantly higher in the thin group. Only 2 patients in the thick group experienced an adverse outcome. Eleven patients underwent multiple procedures for spontaneous leaks. The recurrence rate was 14.5%. All but 1 recurrence occurred in the thin group."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "content": "An abnormally thin tegmen was significantly associated with adverse perioperative outcomes in cases of spontaneous CSF otorrhea. A thick skull base and the presence of an encephalocele may be protective against recurrence. The effect of untreated intracranial hypertension on the results is unknown."
          }
        ]
      },
